# shm-coherence-varidx: real, falsifiable coverage of all three
# `zone_invariant` forms against cache_turbo's own shared counters.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# --------------------
# testkit's own ci/prober/scenarios/shm-coherence is the live-worker leg of the
# `fanout` / `quiesce` / `zone_invariant` lens, and it DELIBERATELY asserts no
# zone_invariant at all. Its rule file says why: the reference module never
# mutates its shm zone on a request path, so against a constant field
#
#   - `coherent  <f>`        is satisfied by any constant;
#   - `at_rest   <f> == N`   is satisfied by a field that was never anything
#                            but N;
#   - `monotonic <f>`        is satisfied because a constant never decreases.
#
# All three would be green there for reasons unrelated to whether their
# comparisons work. That file names cache-turbo's `varidx_inflight` as the
# intended home of the real coverage. This is that home.
#
#
# THE FIELDS, AND WHY EACH FORM READS THE ONE IT DOES
# ---------------------------------------------------
# All five are rendered by src/ngx_http_cache_turbo_probe.c's zone_render hook
# into the probe's "zone" object, straight off the zone's shared struct.
#
#   zone.varidx_inflight -> at_rest
#     Incremented in redis_tag_add_many() immediately before a variant-index
#     write is launched (redis.c:1706), decremented in redis_op_done(), which
#     every completion and every failure path funnels through (redis.c:1715,
#     :3515). Its resting value is 0 and ONLY 0. A launch whose completion
#     never decremented strands it above 0 permanently -- that is COR-5's
#     defect class (PR #443), which had no automated guard before this file.
#
#     It is NOT the monotonic field, and that is not an oversight: it goes both
#     up and down by design, so `monotonic zone.varidx_inflight` would assert a
#     falsehood and fire on correct behaviour. A form that reddens on healthy
#     code is worse than no form.
#
#   zone.lookups -> monotonic
#     hits + misses, summed in the hook because zone_invariant takes ONE field
#     path and cannot compute over two. Both addends are lifetime tallies,
#     only ever ngx_atomic_fetch_add(+1) on the request path, zeroed exactly
#     once when a fresh zone is carved (shm.c:172-173) and never reset
#     thereafter -- a reload inherits the live shm rather than re-initialising
#     it. So the sum is genuinely non-decreasing, and it genuinely MOVES across
#     this scenario's traffic, which is what makes the form falsifiable rather
#     than trivially true.
#
#   zone.ct_digest -> coherent
#     A fold of all four counters. The generic `zone.digest` already exists but
#     is derived from SLAB bookkeeping, so it attests to nginx's allocator, not
#     to this module's counters: a worker whose view of the module's shared
#     struct had diverged would still agree with its siblings on zone.digest
#     while disagreeing about every field above. Folding the module fields into
#     one value makes that disagreement one rule line wide -- the same argument
#     testkit's header makes for zone.digest itself.
#
#
# WHY `quiesce` PRECEDES EVERY `at_rest`
# --------------------------------------
# varidx_inflight is bumped before the SADD reaches the transport and dropped
# when the op completes, and the response is finalized to the client on an
# EARLIER event-loop turn than the one the write completes on -- that
# separation is the entire subject of the counter's own header comment. So the
# counter is provably NOT at rest the instant the last byte reaches the client,
# and an at_rest oracle judged at that moment is timing-dependent: green on an
# idle box, red on a loaded one, and the usual repair is to loosen the bound
# until it stops firing, at which point it asserts nothing. `quiesce` polls for
# two consecutive equal readings and FAILS on expiry rather than degrading to a
# sleep or a skip.
#
#
# WHY `fanout 24` AND NOT LESS
# ----------------------------
# Nothing lets a client choose which worker accepts its connection, so worker
# sampling is probabilistic and a run CAN legitimately land every request on
# one worker. testkit's shm-coherence measured this on the same class of box,
# four workers, sequential fresh connections: `fanout 8` reached 2 distinct
# workers in 7 runs of 8 and never 4; `fanout 24` reached >= 3 in 7 of 7. The
# WIDTH, not the bound, is what buys reliability.
#
# min_workers stays at the meaningful 2. Lowering it to 1 is the tempting fix
# when this flakes and it is a disarmed oracle -- the parser refuses it at load
# time for that reason. If a box cannot reach 2 distinct workers at this width,
# widen the fanout or investigate the accept path; the bound is not the
# adjustable part.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Prime the variant index. This case's job is to MOVE the counters, so the
#    forms below are judged against a zone that actually did work rather than
#    one that sat idle. A Vary-carrying origin response through
#    cache_turbo_auto_vary is what issues the variant-index write.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
name    priming traffic drives real variant-index writes through the zone
from    127.0.0.1
send    GET /varidx/a HTTP/1.1\r\n
send    Host: prober\r\nAccept-Encoding: gzip\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
expect  status=200
expect  body~ct-varidx-payload
pid_may_change
fanout  24 2


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. A second variant of the same base URI. Two variants rather than one
#    because a single variant can be indexed by a write that raced nothing;
#    two make the index set genuinely multi-membered, which is the shape a
#    PURGE's SMEMBERS enumerates and therefore the shape COR-5 is about.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
name    a second variant of the same base URI is indexed too
from    127.0.0.1
send    GET /varidx/a HTTP/1.1\r\n
send    Host: prober\r\nAccept-Encoding: br\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
expect  status=200
expect  body~ct-varidx-payload
pid_may_change
fanout  24 2


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. at_rest -- the COR-5 regression guard, and the reason this file exists.
#
#    `probe zone.varidx_inflight >= 0` above the quiesce is NOT decoration. A
#    field the probe could not render is reported as the -1 unavailable
#    sentinel, and a quiesce would happily settle on two consecutive -1s and
#    then hand at_rest a reading it never actually read. The probe line fails
#    closed on that, so a renamed field or a hook that stopped being called
#    reddens here rather than passing quietly.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
name    the variant-index in-flight counter returns to zero in every worker
from    127.0.0.1
send    GET /varidx/a HTTP/1.1\r\n
send    Host: prober\r\nAccept-Encoding: gzip\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
expect  status=200
expect  body~ct-varidx-payload
pid_may_change
probe   zone.varidx_inflight >= 0
quiesce zone.varidx_inflight 8000
fanout  24 2
zone_invariant at_rest zone.varidx_inflight == 0


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. coherent -- every answering worker read the SAME module state.
#
#    The zone is one mmap shared by every worker, so at rest they cannot
#    honestly disagree about a field of it. A divergence is a torn read, a
#    per-worker copy that drifted, or a write that never reached the shared
#    page. Quiesced on the same counter as case 3 so the digest is folded from
#    a settled zone rather than one mid-write -- an unquiesced coherent would
#    redden on nothing worse than two probes landing either side of a healthy
#    increment.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
name    every answering worker reads the same module counter state
from    127.0.0.1
send    GET /varidx/a HTTP/1.1\r\n
send    Host: prober\r\nAccept-Encoding: gzip\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
expect  status=200
expect  body~ct-varidx-payload
pid_may_change
probe   zone.ct_digest >= 0
quiesce zone.varidx_inflight 8000
fanout  24 2
zone_invariant coherent zone.ct_digest


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. monotonic -- the lifetime lookup tally never decreased across the fan.
#
#    No quiesce here, deliberately. monotonic asserts a property of the
#    SEQUENCE of readings, and the readings this fan takes while traffic is
#    flowing are the interesting ones: a settled zone would make the sequence
#    constant, and a constant sequence never decreases, which is the tautology
#    this whole file exists to avoid. Judged live, `lookups` climbs across the
#    24 legs and a decrease is a real finding.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
name    the lifetime lookup tally never decreases across the fan
from    127.0.0.1
send    GET /varidx/a HTTP/1.1\r\n
send    Host: prober\r\nAccept-Encoding: gzip\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
expect  status=200
expect  body~ct-varidx-payload
pid_may_change
probe   zone.lookups >= 1
fanout  24 2
zone_invariant monotonic zone.lookups
